WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.460
Let’s play a game.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:28.789
If I say ‘Cuba’ and then I mention a leader
who ratified a constitution backed by the

00:00:28.789 --> 00:00:33.410
people, instituted land reforms, legalised
the Communist Party, cooperated with the trade

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:37.370
unions and actively opposed Fascism …
[each point appears on screen with a ‘ping!’:

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:38.370
Constitution
Land reforms

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:39.370
Communist party
Trade unions

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:40.370
Vs Fascism]
… who comes to mind?

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:41.370
Fidel Castro?

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:42.370
Che Guevara?

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:43.370
Yeah, sure.

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:46.050
But one man did it all 19 years before they
came to power.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:53.990
None other than their sworn rival, Cuban dictator
Fulgencio Batista.

00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:56.530
Confused?

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:57.530
You should!

00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:02.000
But keep on watching to learn more about the
complex life of a man who rose from the very

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:08.360
bottom of the social scale, ended up ruling
the 4th largest economy in Latin America,

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:12.960
made an alliance with the Mafia, escaped an
assassination attempt thanks to Santería

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:19.740
magical practices and eventually fled at dawn
with three cargo planes filled with loot.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:26.220
Humble origins
Fulgencio Batista y Zaldívar,

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:31.460
[Full-hen-theo Batista ee Thull-dee-var]
Was born on the 16th of January 1901 from

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:36.450
a family of impoverished farmers in Banes,
Cuba, not far from the estate in which Fidel

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:40.270
Castro would come to life in a wealthy household
of land owners.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:45.020
This was only 3 years after Cuba had gained
freedom from Spain’s colonial dominance,

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:46.820
following the Hispano-American War.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:52.780
In 1898 the US congress had passed the Teller
amendment granting full independence to Cuba,

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:58.380
but this was partially revoked by the Platt
amendment of 1901 which gave power to the

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:03.260
US Government to intervene in the nation island
if American interests were threatened.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:08.580
And Washington had plenty of motivations to
be involved in Cuban politics – 30 to 50

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:13.629
Million to be precise: as early as 1896 President
Grover Cleveland stated that American investments

00:02:13.629 --> 00:02:19.019
in Cuban sugar cane plantations, railroads,
mining and other enterprises amounted to 30

00:02:19.019 --> 00:02:24.420
to 50 MLN $. That’s up to 1.5 Billion in
today’s money.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:30.150
As a result, Cuban politics, economy and society
were often in turmoil, badly managed by a

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:34.829
string of corrupt Presidents who ruled to
please the land owner elite with substantial

00:02:34.829 --> 00:02:35.930
US backing.

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:41.689
Just as an example, consider the story of
the PIC, Partido Independiente de Color – or

00:02:41.689 --> 00:02:47.129
Coloured Independent Party, founded by Afro-Cuban
veterans of the war against Spain.

00:02:47.129 --> 00:02:51.870
In 1908 the Party won enough votes to threaten
the rule of the then President Gomez, to which

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:53.810
he reacted by disbanding it.

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:59.389
As a pretext, he cited a law which forbade
the creation of parties based on race – a

00:02:59.389 --> 00:03:03.159
law which was made to protect the ruling white
Cuban elite.

00:03:03.159 --> 00:03:08.900
When the PIC staged a revolt, Gomez launched
a bloody crackdown, backed by the regular

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:12.640
Army and US troops sent to protect American
interests.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:17.319
5000 Afro-Cubans died in the struggle, many
of them lynched by mobs who had been stirred

00:03:17.319 --> 00:03:19.419
to attack any citizen of colour.

00:03:19.419 --> 00:03:23.709
This was the country in which young Fulgencio
tried to make his way.

00:03:23.709 --> 00:03:28.529
Batista had a mixed ancestry: Spanish, African,
Chinese, and native Caribbean.

00:03:28.529 --> 00:03:34.590
How could he, an impoverished rural half-caste,
a mulatto[TA1] , how could he possibly leave

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:38.459
his mark in a Cuba dominated by white land
owners?

00:03:38.459 --> 00:03:43.709
One such way was the Army, which he joined
in 1921 as a private, more out of necessity

00:03:43.709 --> 00:03:45.879
than as a vocational choice.

00:03:45.879 --> 00:03:49.400
Young Fulgencio had ambitions, in fact, and
his service in the army helped him support

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:52.340
his studies as a stenographer and a journalist.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:57.019
While stationed in the Wajay suburb of Havana,
Private Batista met and fell in love with

00:03:57.019 --> 00:03:59.169
a girl called Elisa Godinez Gomez.

00:03:59.169 --> 00:04:03.519
She came from a similar rural background and
had moved to the city after her father got

00:04:03.519 --> 00:04:05.530
a job in a psychiatric hospital.

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:10.230
Fulgencio and Elisa married on the 10th of
July 1926 and they would go on to have three

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:13.769
children: Mirta, Fulgencio Ruben and Elisa
Aleida.

00:04:13.769 --> 00:04:18.549
But apparently Batista was not fully satisfied
by married life and went on to have at least

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:23.949
two officially recognised affairs: the first
one, with the mother of a girl called Fermina,

00:04:23.949 --> 00:04:27.220
born out of wedlock, whom he would legally
recognise.

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:31.750
The second one with a lady called Marta Fernandez,
but more on this later.

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:37.860
In 1928 the private became Sergeant Batista
and was assigned to the barracks of Camp Columbia,

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:39.060
in Havana.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:42.729
It was here that he first made contact with
fellow servicemen who secretly opposed the

00:04:42.729 --> 00:04:46.500
rule of President Gerardo Machado [Her-ar-doe
Match-add-aw] . Thanks to his personal charisma

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:51.060
he developed a large network of followers
which placed him at the centre of the conspirator

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:52.780
circles.

00:04:52.780 --> 00:05:00.030
Sergeants in Revolt
President Machado had been in power since

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:04.860
1925 and had started with good intentions:
putting an end to the Platt Agreement and

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:09.950
US interference as well as transforming Cuba
into the “Switzerland of the Americas”.

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:17.039
But a recession in 1928, worsened by the Wall
Street crash of 1929, led to political instability

00:05:17.039 --> 00:05:21.660
and growing opposition from students, trade
unions and the military.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:28.259
In July 1933, US envoy Sumner Welles went
‘full Platt’ on Machado and made him step

00:05:28.259 --> 00:05:32.680
aside, helping install new President Carlos
Manuel de Cespedes [Thess-payh-dayhs] . The

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:37.729
new guy had just finished unpacking his brief
case when he was ousted by the “Revolt of

00:05:37.729 --> 00:05:39.780
the Sergeants” in September.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:43.750
By the way: if at any point during this video
you lose count of the Cuban presidents coming

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:46.520
and going, don’t worry – so did the Cubans.

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:50.699
But back to the Sergeants: their leader was
none other than Fulgencio Batista, who had

00:05:50.699 --> 00:05:56.449
seized the occasion to rise through the ranks
and stick it to Uncle Sam in one single action.

00:05:56.449 --> 00:06:01.150
And you must give credit to the man in this
occasion: an army Sergeant has formal authority

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:06.580
over 10 to 30 soldiers at best, that is why
military coups are carried out by Colonels

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:09.960
or Generals with thousands of troops under
their orders.

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:15.270
And yet he had managed to lead a successful
revolt based only on charisma and informal

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:16.270
leadership.

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:20.759
Batista and the Sergeants replaced Cespedes
with new President Professor Ramón Grau San

00:06:20.759 --> 00:06:23.449
Martín [Grr-ou as in ‘ouch’] while Fulgencio
gave himself a promotion and became Chief

00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:26.629
of Staff of the Army with the rank of Colonel.

00:06:26.629 --> 00:06:33.660
Batista the Puppet Master
In the following years the newly appointed

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:39.039
Colonel Batista would hold power in the shadows,
pulling strings to make or break presidencies.

00:06:39.039 --> 00:06:43.909
And to further his plans he did not mind cooperating
with the US from the start.

00:06:43.909 --> 00:06:49.409
For example, in late 1933 Professor Grau launched
some attempts at reform but his time in office

00:06:49.409 --> 00:06:53.389
was characterised by social instability and
constant opposition.

00:06:53.389 --> 00:06:59.289
After only 100 days Grau received the deluxe
Platt treatment by Sumner Welles with Batista’s

00:06:59.289 --> 00:07:05.039
full support and cooperation: Cuba needed
stability and Grau was not the right man.

00:07:05.039 --> 00:07:08.909
Batista then handed over the presidency on
Colonel Carlos Mendieta [Men-dee-ay-tah],

00:07:08.909 --> 00:07:14.129
who had the approval of most factions, and
most importantly of the U.S.

00:07:14.129 --> 00:07:19.099
In June 1934 President Roosevelt concluded
a treaty with Cuba that finally abrogated

00:07:19.099 --> 00:07:21.319
once and for all the Platt Amendment.

00:07:21.319 --> 00:07:26.039
This was not the end of American interference
in Cuban affairs but at least it wasn’t

00:07:26.039 --> 00:07:27.509
formally sanctioned any more.

00:07:27.509 --> 00:07:32.530
FDR may have also realised that political
factions in Cuba had been exploiting the amendment

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:38.440
to their advantage, by creating unrest to
trigger American interventions on their side.

00:07:38.440 --> 00:08:01.039
More presidents

00:08:01.039 --> 00:08:24.879
came

00:08:24.879 --> 00:08:36.340
and went under Batista: Barnet.

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:47.460
[Ping on screen: Barnet – 6 months]
Arias.

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:54.090
[Arias – 6 months]
Bru.

00:08:54.090 --> 00:09:07.360
[Bru – 3 years 10 months]
Well done, Bru!

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:24.660
Out of the shadows
President Bru’s term in office takes us

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:27.470
to October 1940, a time for new elections.

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:32.630
Tired of being the power in the shadows, Batista
finally decided to run for the presidency,

00:09:32.630 --> 00:09:36.890
supported by a coalition which included the
Cuban Communist Party, which himself had legalised

00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:38.200
in the previous decade.

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:42.560
His main opponent in the 1940 election was
an old acquaintance, Professor Grau.

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:48.870
And this is when Colonel Fulgencio Batista,
the former and future leader of revolts and

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:54.410
coups, stepped into power as a democratically
elected representative of the people.

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:59.070
One can only imagine the sense of achievement
felt by this son of farm labourers who became

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:03.800
a soldier, then a Sergeant, a Colonel, the
driving force behind Cuba’s string of rulers,

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:08.840
and finally its first non-white President
in a country historically ruled by sugar cane

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:11.140
tycoons of European descent.

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:15.780
Batista’s presidential term, from 1940 to
1944 is generally viewed in a positive light

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:17.070
by historians.

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:21.720
During this period Batista oversaw the drafting
of a new constitution: a progressive document

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:26.380
which called for government intervention in
the economy and provided a social safety net.

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:31.600
His policies included an economic and agrarian
reform, cooperation with the trade unions,

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:37.250
an expansion of the educational system and
an active opposition to Fascism, made explicit

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:41.850
by declaring war on Germany and Italy on the
8th of December 1941.

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:45.950
All in all, it was a period of growth, stability
and democratic development for the island

00:10:45.950 --> 00:10:46.950
nation.

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:51.570
Batista ruled well, although he did take the
opportunity to enrich himself.

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:56.060
After his term ended in 1944, Batista stepped
aside, preferring to sponsor new candidate

00:10:56.060 --> 00:11:02.220
Carlos Saladrigas, who was defeated by Professor
Grau – yes, him again!

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:06.790
Batista soon left the country and lived for
a while in Florida, where he increased his

00:11:06.790 --> 00:11:10.310
wealth by investing the huge sums he had acquired
in Cuba.

00:11:10.310 --> 00:11:15.240
The following year, 1945, Batista finally
divorced from Elisa and married his long-time

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:19.360
mistress, Marta Fernandez Miranda, who would
give him four more children.

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:24.230
During his years of absence Grau’s administration
became known for its corruption and irresponsibility

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:29.270
– a great disappointment for the Cuban electorate
who saw Grau as a ‘pure’ hero from the

00:11:29.270 --> 00:11:30.690
1933 revolt.

00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:36.720
This malcontent paved the way for Batista’s
return to the island, this time as a Senator,

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:39.820
in 1948.

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:41.370
Golpe!

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:49.270
From 1948 to 1952 Cuba was run by elected
President Prio Socarras.

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:54.660
Like Grau, he was an ambitious and idealistic
leader, whose agenda got marred by corruption

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:56.740
and economic stagnation.

00:11:56.740 --> 00:12:01.691
When new elections were called to take place
in June1952, Batista run for office for a

00:12:01.691 --> 00:12:02.691
second time.

00:12:02.691 --> 00:12:07.050
Anticipating a defeat, the former Colonel
launched a bloodless golpe, a coup, on the

00:12:07.050 --> 00:12:08.770
10th of March 1952.

00:12:08.770 --> 00:12:11.790
Washington immediately recognized the new
Cuban government.

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:15.910
It became clear from the start that Batista’s
approach to running the country had radically

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:18.000
change from his early presidency.

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:23.380
First, he suspended the 1940 Constitution
he himself had instituted and cancelled most

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:25.570
of the remaining civil liberties.

00:12:25.570 --> 00:12:30.380
Whereas in 1940 he had sought the alliance
of trade unions and the Communist Party, he

00:12:30.380 --> 00:12:34.100
now backed the wealthy landowners who owned
sugar plantations.

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:39.100
The gap between rich and poor increased and
government corruption was rife.

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:43.690
Whereas in 1940 his accumulation of wealth
appeared as a by-product of his populist rule,

00:12:43.690 --> 00:12:47.420
now the reaping of profits was his only evident
goal.

00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:51.300
And finally, whereas in his previous years
in power he had sought cooperation with the

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:56.880
US only as a means to further his political
goals, he now welcomed with open arms the

00:12:56.880 --> 00:13:00.850
intervention of both America’s government
and its private sector.

00:13:00.850 --> 00:13:05.450
By the late 1950s, the US corporations owned
90% of Cuban mines [ping]

00:13:05.450 --> 00:13:08.190
80% public utilities
50% of the railways

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:13.310
40% of the sugar production and
25% of the bank deposits

00:13:13.310 --> 00:13:18.250
In an effort to boost tourism, Batista also
invited a different kind of American entrepreneurship

00:13:18.250 --> 00:13:23.040
to invest in an industry as Cuban as cigar
and rum production: gambling

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:32.680
Batista and the Mob
If you have seen the Godfather part II you

00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:37.610
will surely remember a long section set in
Havana, in which Michael Corleone meets gangster

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:42.450
Hyman Roth, who runs the profitable gambling
business in Cuba in cahoots with the Government.

00:13:42.450 --> 00:13:48.120
This Roth character is inspired by a real-life
gangster, Meyer Lansky, a high-ranking mobster

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:49.550
of the Jewish Mafia.

00:13:49.550 --> 00:13:55.000
Lansky and legendary Italo-American mafioso
Lucky Luciano had been cooperating with Batista

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:56.720
since his Florida years.

00:13:56.720 --> 00:14:01.100
When the dictator had seized power in 1952
he had realised that to attract the right

00:14:01.100 --> 00:14:06.250
kind of tourism and investments he needed
to clean up Havana’s Casinos and red-light

00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:07.250
district.

00:14:07.250 --> 00:14:09.380
Who could do a better job than the Mob?

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:14.010
Lansky in particular had a real talent to
organise complex operations and have businesses

00:14:14.010 --> 00:14:15.010
run like clockwork.

00:14:15.010 --> 00:14:19.730
He took charge of the casinos, becoming a
sort of unofficial – but much needed – Minister

00:14:19.730 --> 00:14:20.730
of Gambling.

00:14:20.730 --> 00:14:26.140
What did Lansky, Luciano, and other mobsters
such as Santo Trafficante get in return?

00:14:26.140 --> 00:14:30.510
Well, for starters, they got loads of money
from Batista’ Government, but most of all

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:34.970
they had free rein in dealing cocaine in and
out of the island.

00:14:34.970 --> 00:14:38.800
This was a time when VIP members at one of
the smarter Havana nightclubs had their own

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:41.600
lockers to hold their cocaine stash.

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:42.600
Author T.J.

00:14:42.600 --> 00:14:44.350
English described the Havana of the period
as

00:14:44.350 --> 00:14:49.720
“A volatile mix of Monte Carlo, Casablanca
and the ancient city of Cadiz all rolled into

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:50.720
one.

00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:55.370
A bitches' brew of high-stakes gambling, secret
revolutionary plots, violent repression and

00:14:55.370 --> 00:14:59.030
gangsterism”
And yet, the unholy alliance of Batista with

00:14:59.030 --> 00:15:05.280
the Mob was just a minor symptom of the broader
illnesses plaguing Cuba in the 1950s: government

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:11.030
corruption, unemployment, exploitation of
low paid female labour, USA interference and

00:15:11.030 --> 00:15:15.850
the toxic dependence on a single-crop economy
– sugar cane.

00:15:15.850 --> 00:15:22.140
This system was due for a collapse, and a
young lawyer was going to accelerate its demise.

00:15:22.140 --> 00:15:25.410
Enter Fidel.

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:30.320
Viva la Revolución!

00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:35.070
As public dissent increased, Batista tightened
his control over the media by way of censorship

00:15:35.070 --> 00:15:40.010
and created an anti-Communist secret police
to intimidate the opposition by the means

00:15:40.010 --> 00:15:42.920
of violence, torture and executions.

00:15:42.920 --> 00:15:48.250
Between 10,000 to 20,000 people were murdered
under the Batista regime, with financial and

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:50.340
military support from Washington.

00:15:50.340 --> 00:15:55.630
Historian Arthur Schlesinger Jr., who would
become an aide to John Kennedy, analysed Batista’s

00:15:55.630 --> 00:15:59.310
Cuba in these terms:
“The corruption of the government, the brutality

00:15:59.310 --> 00:16:03.850
of the police, the regime’s indifference
to the needs of the people for education,

00:16:03.850 --> 00:16:10.090
medical care, housing, for social justice
and economic justice is an open invitation

00:16:10.090 --> 00:16:15.170
to revolution”
The invitation was RSVP’ed by the July 26th

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:20.060
Movement, led by lawyer and politician Fidel
Castro.

00:16:20.060 --> 00:16:24.700
Quick break here: we have covered extensively
the stages of the Revolution in our videos

00:16:24.700 --> 00:16:27.980
about Fidel Castro and his ally, Comandante
Ernesto ‘Che’ Guevara.

00:16:27.980 --> 00:16:31.880
If you want to know more about the fight against
Batista’s regime from their perspective,

00:16:31.880 --> 00:16:36.980
make sure you check our videos, you will find
the links in the description below.

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:41.730
Today we are going to look at this revolutionary
struggle from the perspective of the losing

00:16:41.730 --> 00:16:43.660
side: Batista’s.

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:48.840
And more specifically: how did it happen that
Batista lost against the July 26 Movement,

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:54.370
despite having full control of the armed forces
and facing a less formidable opposition?

00:16:54.370 --> 00:16:59.500
According to writers Norberto Fuentes and
Carlos Alberto Montaner, Batista made three

00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:04.260
strategic blunders in the fight against Castro
which would bring about his defeat.

00:17:04.260 --> 00:17:10.270
On the 26th of July 1953 Fidel, his brother
Raul and other 160 men attacked the Moncada

00:17:10.270 --> 00:17:13.920
army barracks in Santiago, hoping to spark
a general uprising.

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:14.920
They didn’t.

00:17:14.920 --> 00:17:18.150
Most of the insurgents were killed and the
Castro brothers were arrested.

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:25.589
Batista had Fidel in his grip and yet he let
him go away during a general amnesty in 1955

00:17:25.589 --> 00:17:26.670
– first blunder.

00:17:26.670 --> 00:17:28.410
Fidel fled to Mexico.

00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:33.370
On the 2nd of December 1956 he returned to
Cuba, aboard the yacht Granma followed by

00:17:33.370 --> 00:17:36.740
81 revolutionaries, hoping to spark a general
uprising.

00:17:36.740 --> 00:17:37.740
They didn’t.

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:42.650
Most of the insurgents were killed, but Castro
and few others made it to the jungles of the

00:17:42.650 --> 00:17:43.930
Sierra Maestra.

00:17:43.930 --> 00:17:48.970
Batista’s army, instead of pushing the invaders
back into the sea, chased them to the interior

00:17:48.970 --> 00:17:55.140
of the island, giving them a shot at a successful
guerrilla campaign – second blunder.

00:17:55.140 --> 00:17:59.670
As Castro’s followers increased in numbers
and their actions became more effective, Batista

00:17:59.670 --> 00:18:02.110
missed the opportunity to finish them quickly.

00:18:02.110 --> 00:18:06.370
In Montaner’s words:
“"Batista does not finish Fidel out of greed...

00:18:06.370 --> 00:18:08.540
His is a government of thieves.

00:18:08.540 --> 00:18:13.150
To have this small guerrilla band in the mountains
is to his advantage, so that he can order

00:18:13.150 --> 00:18:18.000
special Defense expenditures that he can steal."

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:19.000
Third Blunder.

00:18:19.000 --> 00:18:23.760
By the time Batista sent a significant force
of ten thousand soldiers Castro had become

00:18:23.760 --> 00:18:30.400
too strong and the regular army too corrupt
to be effective.

00:18:30.400 --> 00:18:35.920
Secret door
But the 26th of July Movement was not the

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:38.480
only foe the regime had to contend with.

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:44.230
Another organisation, called Directorio Revolucionario
- Revolutionary Directorate – had Batista

00:18:44.230 --> 00:18:45.500
in their sights.

00:18:45.500 --> 00:18:49.480
Despite Castro’s disagreement the directorate
planned a direct attack on the Presidential

00:18:49.480 --> 00:18:52.720
Palace in Havana, with the aim of assassinating
Batista.

00:18:52.720 --> 00:18:58.490
On the 13th of March 1957 a commando of the
directorate launched an assault on the Palace,

00:18:58.490 --> 00:19:03.030
surrounding the building and fighting their
way through its rooms with gunfire and hand

00:19:03.030 --> 00:19:04.030
grenades.

00:19:04.030 --> 00:19:08.610
They had chosen to attack in the late afternoon,
a time which Batista notoriously spent working

00:19:08.610 --> 00:19:10.820
in his office on the second floor.

00:19:10.820 --> 00:19:13.920
But when they forced their way into the office
… Batista had disappeared.

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:17.520
The attack ended in failure and incarceration
for those involved.

00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:22.120
It later appeared that Batista had installed
a secret door in his office, well hidden behind

00:19:22.120 --> 00:19:23.280
a plastic panel.

00:19:23.280 --> 00:19:28.020
Upon hearing the incoming shots and explosions,
he had simply slipped through the secret passage

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:29.550
and escaped to safety.

00:19:29.550 --> 00:19:31.620
But why would he need such a passage?

00:19:31.620 --> 00:19:34.250
Personal safety would be a perfectly normal
explanation.

00:19:34.250 --> 00:19:36.560
But we have a better one.

00:19:36.560 --> 00:19:41.470
Natalia Bolivar is an expert of Santeria,
the religious practices of African origin

00:19:41.470 --> 00:19:43.880
practiced by some minorities in Cuba.

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:48.010
According to her, Batista had a fascination
with these type of rites, one of them being

00:19:48.010 --> 00:19:49.380
“The Letter of the Year”.

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:52.170
In this ceremony the Babalawos, or priests,
determine which ‘odu’, a ‘sign’ or

00:19:52.170 --> 00:19:53.800
letter, will dominate a certain year.

00:19:53.800 --> 00:19:58.720
Batista was informed that the odu for 1957
was Obbara Mey – meaning

00:19:58.720 --> 00:20:02.430
“The King must find an escape from the places
where he dwells.

00:20:02.430 --> 00:20:06.321
A secret escape”
And so, Batista was inspired to install the

00:20:06.321 --> 00:20:09.650
secret door just one month prior to the assassination
attempt.

00:20:09.650 --> 00:20:14.270
He had managed to escape that specific attack,
but he was not going to be safe for long.

00:20:14.270 --> 00:20:18.350
Fidel, Raul and Che were getting closer and
closer to Havana.

00:20:18.350 --> 00:20:20.460
Farewell to Havana
According to writer T.J.

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:24.890
English Batista spent the last weeks of the
revolution secluded in his palace.

00:20:24.890 --> 00:20:29.880
With his sanity almost gone, the dictator
would spend his days gorging on fine foods,

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:34.450
interrupting his banquets only to watch American
horror movies and to throw up in his garden.

00:20:34.450 --> 00:20:39.540
On the 27th of December 1958 a train carrying
a much-needed load of weapons and ammunition

00:20:39.540 --> 00:20:43.550
for the regular army was captured by Che Guevara
in Santa Clara.

00:20:43.550 --> 00:20:46.880
Batista realised that his defences were now
doomed.

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:51.540
He abdicated his presidency and in the early
hours of the 1st of January 1959 he fled from

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:53.250
Havana to the Dominican Republic.

00:20:53.250 --> 00:20:58.210
He brought along 40 family members and loyal
supporters, as well as the bare necessities:

00:20:58.210 --> 00:21:05.280
$300 to $700 Million dollars’ worth of embezzled
funds, pieces of art and other assets, stashed

00:21:05.280 --> 00:21:06.540
in 3 cargo planes.

00:21:06.540 --> 00:21:12.210
He applied for asylum to the US, but Washington
has long withdrawn its support to the losing

00:21:12.210 --> 00:21:13.630
side of the revolution.

00:21:13.630 --> 00:21:19.220
Batista relocated instead to Portugal and
finally to Marbella in Francoist Spain, which

00:21:19.220 --> 00:21:25.650
in those years was a common sanctuary for
military dictators, fascists and escaped Nazis.

00:21:25.650 --> 00:21:29.800
Batista lived his last years in splendour,
surrounded by the children from his second

00:21:29.800 --> 00:21:33.440
marriage who described him as
“A man who never raised his voice … he

00:21:33.440 --> 00:21:37.230
always displayed great affection and tenderness
to us and our mother.

00:21:37.230 --> 00:21:40.150
He was in general likable and sympathetic
to everybody.

00:21:40.150 --> 00:21:45.930
History, though, has judged him otherwise”
Fulgencio Batista died on the 6th of August

00:21:45.930 --> 00:21:47.390
1973 of a heart attack.

00:21:47.390 --> 00:21:51.380
A team of assassins sent by Castro were due
to kill him on the 8th.

00:21:51.380 --> 00:21:56.130
We would like to hear your judgment of today’s
protagonist, please leave your views in the

00:21:56.130 --> 00:21:57.300
comments below.

00:21:57.300 --> 00:22:01.310
Was Batista the first, true, effective revolutionary
that Cuba badly needed?

00:22:01.310 --> 00:22:21.560
Or was he simply a social climber motivated
purely by greed all along?

00:22:21.560 --> 00:22:23.130
As usual … thank you for watching!

